 **Commands:** `omarchy restart waybar`, `omarchy refresh waybar`, `omarchy toggle waybar`
 
+**If a laptop/docked profile-switcher is in play, `config.jsonc` may be a generated file, not the source of truth.** Check for a script like `~/.config/waybar/scripts/waybar-apply-profile.sh` (often wired into `~/.config/hypr/autostart.conf` and a monitor-watch script) before editing `config.jsonc` directly — on a Jack-style setup it overwrites `config.jsonc` from `config.laptop.jsonc` / `config.docked.jsonc` on every monitor plug/unplug event, silently reverting any edit made only to `config.jsonc`. Edit the per-profile template(s) that match, then re-apply (`cp` the template over `config.jsonc`, or just trigger the profile script) and `omarchy restart waybar`.
+
+**Waybar group `drawer` with `"click-to-reveal": true` is unreliable** — it's a [known upstream bug](https://github.com/Alexays/Waybar/issues/3625) across many Waybar versions where clicks don't register at all. Prefer the default hover-reveal (omit `click-to-reveal` or set it `false`) unless you've confirmed click actually works on the installed version.
+
+**Debugging waybar interactively:** there's usually no systemd unit for it (`journalctl --user -u waybar` returns nothing) and the normal restart script discards stdout/stderr. To capture real logs, launch it explicitly with output redirected inside the exec string (a bare `hyprctl dispatch exec "waybar --log-level trace > file"` from a detached shell won't inherit your redirection):
+```bash
+hyprctl dispatch exec "uwsm-app -- bash -c 'waybar --log-level trace > /path/to/log 2>&1'"
+```
+`hyprctl` itself needs `HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE` set — from a plain Bash tool call (not already inside the graphical session) find it with `ls -t /run/user/$(id -u)/hypr/ | head -1` and export it first.
+
+**`hyprctl dispatch movecursor x y` is not reliable for testing hover-triggered UI.** It repositions the compositor's cursor but does not reliably emit the `wl_pointer` enter/motion events a GTK layer-shell client (like Waybar) needs to fire hover states — a drawer that doesn't visibly reveal under `movecursor` is inconclusive, not proof of a bug. Confirm hover behavior with the user's real mouse, or install `ydotool`/`python-evdev` for genuine synthetic input if scripted testing is required.
+
+**Custom waybar module scripts that cache (`~/.cache/waybar-status/*.json` with a TTL) will silently show stale data if the underlying input changes but isn't part of the cache key or a refresh signal.** Example: a weather module cached by TTL alone kept showing the old city for up to 15 min after `~/.config/omarchy/location.conf` changed, because the cache didn't know the city had changed. Fix pattern: embed the relevant input (e.g. city) in the cached JSON and invalidate on mismatch regardless of TTL, and have whatever script changes the input also `rm` the cache + `pkill -RTMIN+<n> waybar` (with a matching `"signal": <n>` on the module) so the bar updates immediately instead of waiting for the next poll interval.
+
+**Spotify on native Wayland (`spotify-launcher`) + HiDPI:** don't add `--force-device-scale-factor` to `~/.config/spotify-launcher.conf`'s `extra_arguments` if the compositor is already applying output scale (e.g. `scale = 2` in `monitors.conf`) — it double-scales and makes the whole UI oversized, not just fixes popups. If Spotify's context menus render broken/stretched on a scaled display, that's a separate, still-unresolved-as-of-2026 Electron/CEF popup-positioning issue on Hyprland; forcing scale factor is not the fix.
+
